==English==

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|fro|mussette}}, diminutive of {{m|fro|musse}}, {{m|fro|muce||a hiding place}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A small [[hole]] or [[gap]] through which a wild animal passes; a [[muse]].
#* '''1593''', William Shakespeare, ''[[w:Venus_and_Adonis_(Shakespeare_poem)|Venus and Adonis]]'', lines 679-684:
#*: And when thou hast on foot the purblind hare,
#*: Mark the poor wretch, to overshoot his troubles
#*: How he outruns the wind and with what care
#*: He cranks and crosses with a thousand doubles:
#*: The many '''musets''' through the which he goes
#*: Are like a labyrinth to amaze his foes.

{{Webster 1913}}

